officialrecordso0009unse_djvu.txt. | Jamestown Island | nd a third recently burned and sunk. During the night the Commodore Morris also grounded, and the Commodore Jones , to avoid doing so, was compelled to anchor. In consequence of these delays, the gunboats and transports were not able to make the connection at Jamestown Island with the troops, as proposed by General Iveyes in the enclosed. The ironclads reached that point on the next day, the 11th instant, at about noon. I com¬ municated that p. m. in writing and in person with General Keyes, and the next morning, the 12th instant,
officialrecordso0009unse_djvu.txt. | Jamestown Island | d at 9: 30 came to anchor off Jamestown [Island] wharf. Abstract log of the TJ. S. S. Commodore Morris, June 9-11, 1863. June 9, 1863. — At 10 : 30 a. m. large bodies of troops commenced marching on Williamsburg. June 11. — At 4:30 a. m. proceeded up river to Jamestown Island wharf. At 6:30 other steamers joined us. At 7:30 left the wharf; saw a body of rebel cavalry on west shore of river. Fired two shells from 30-pounder; rebels signaling from shore. Proceeded on up river. Fired a number of shell at rebel signal stations. At 10:
