warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | carried the main line. He describes the rebel artillery fire as very heavy. He expected to assault this line jus.t before dark. Hancock is within three miles of Smith. C. A. DANA. Hon. E. M. Stanton, Secretary of War. City Point, June 16 , 1864 — 7 a. m. (Yia Jamestown Island 11.45 a. m. Received 4.45 a. m. 17th.) At 7.20 p. m. yesterday Smith assaulted and carried the principal line of the enemy before Petersburg, taking 13 cannon, several stand of colors, and between 300 and 400 prisoners. This line is two miles from Petersburg.
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | up and took position on Smith’s left at 3 a. m. to-day. There was heavy firing in that direction there from 5 to 6. No report yet. C. A. DANA. Hon. E, M. Stanton. Chap. LII.) THE RICHMOND CAMPAIGN. 21 Heights South of Petersburg, June 16 , 1864 — 8 a. m. (Yia Jamestown Island 12.30 p. m. Received 4 p. m. 17tli.) Tlie success of Smitli last niglit was of the most important character. He carried these heights, which were defended by works of the most formidable character, and this gives us perfect command of the city and railroad. T
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | or Duane, is of the most admirable solidity. After Duane had nearly finished it Benham came up and took charge. C. A. DANA. Hon. Edwin M. Stanton, Secretary of War. 22 OPERATIONS IN SE. VA. AND N. C. [Chap. LIT. City Point, Va., June 16 , 1864 — 1 p. on. (Via Jamestown Island 11.45 p. m. Peeeived 5.30 a. m. 17th.) The Richmond Whig- of yesterday says that General Stahel, with 2,000 cavalry, lias passed Avest through Robertson’s Gap, apparently to destroy the Tennessee railroad. The infantry force Avhicli had been at Amherst Court-
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | on the field. The same paper lias a dispatch from Atlanta stating that General Polk was killed by a cannon-ball at 11 a. m. Johnston, Hardee, and Jackson Avere with him when he fell. C. A. DAM. Hon. E. M. Stanton. City Point, June 16 , 1864 — 4.15 p. on. (Via Jamestown Island 11.45 p. m. Received 4.50 a. m. 17th.) General Butler reports from Bermuda Hundred that the enemy have abandoned the works in the front of that place. His troops are now engaged in tearing up the railroad between* Petersburg and Richmond. Two divisions of the
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | gaged in tearing up the railroad between* Petersburg and Richmond. Two divisions of the Sixth Corps, now coming up on steamers from Bout hat’s Landing, Avill be sent to support Butler. C. A. DANA. Hon. E. M. Stanton. City Point, June 17 , 1864 — 9 a. on. (Via Jamestown Island 8p. m. Received 1.30 a. m. 18tli.) I find that I was wrong yesterday in regard to the points of the com¬ pass. The works taken by Smith on Wednesday evening are on the east and northeast of Petersburg, extending across the line of the City Point railroad. Aft
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | Jamestown Island | , Va ., December [31], 1864 . On the morning of the 14th [June] a large proportion of the medical officers, the commissary, and the quartermaster, together with the purveying department and most of the hospital property, started down the Pamunkey en route for Jamestown Island, in the James River, which had been designated in your letter of the 12th as the probable rendezvous until further orders. A temporary delay was occasioned at Yorktown by the great reluctance of the captains of the purveying steamers Planter, Farmer, and Hugh
warofrebellion401unit_djvu.txt | pontoon bridge[^.]{0,80}taken  |  Bridge at 9.30 a. m., and immediately commenced crossing, General Birney in advance, having been directed to pass First and Second Divisions to permit them to cook breakfast. 11 a. m., wrote note, by direction of General Hancock, to General Gibbon to protect pontoon bridge over Chickahominy until it was taken up. Marched rapidly all day. Head of column reached James Biver, near Wilcox’s Landing, at 5.30 p. m. Corps formed line of battle for the night. Prepa¬ rations making for transporting the troops over the James to-morrow. June 14, 1864. — 11.10 a. m., Birney’s tr
